{
  "statement": {
    "index": 1,
    "modal": "Must",
    "text": "judge-jev MUST provide a mode that reports, for one requirement (REQ),\nnon-functional requirement (NFR) or architecture decision record (ADR),\nthe verdict of that record kind's quality decision tree."
  },
  "record": {
    "id": "REQ-JEV-0101",
    "title": "Record quality mode",
    "status": "Proposed",
    "version": "0.2.1",
    "created": "2026-11-07",
    "last_updated": "2026-11-08",
    "owner": "Rand Lee",
    "supersedes": null,
    "superseded_by": null,
    "requirement_statement": {
      "text": "Definitions: Jev is TypeSafe's hosted judge model: it answers each\nmultiple-choice question about a text with one choice and a confidence from\n0 to 1. A Jev request is one call to it. Raptor is the requirements tool\nthat imports Markdown records into its record store and serves them to other\ntools (ADR-JEV-0002). Diagnostics are the problems Raptor's import reports\nfor a record, and the structure it checks is the record format. A quality\ndecision tree is a data file of prompts (questions to Jev) in waves; a wave\nis one set of questions, and its reason is the report of its questions and\nJev's answers. A per-statement wave asks its questions once for each MUST\nand MUST NOT statement. A verdict rule is a rule in the tree file that turns\na wave's answers into a verdict (`yes` or `no`). The walk is the sequence of\nwaves from the first to the one that gives the verdict; its decision-tree\npath is the list of those waves' ids. A consumer is a repository that uses\njudge-jev.",
      "statements": [
        {
          "modal": "Must",
          "text": "judge-jev MUST provide a mode that reports, for one requirement (REQ),\nnon-functional requirement (NFR) or architecture decision record (ADR),\nthe verdict of that record kind's quality decision tree."
        },
        {
          "modal": "Must",
          "text": "The mode MUST judge only records that Raptor imported without\ndiagnostics."
        },
        {
          "modal": "Must",
          "text": "The mode MUST run as a decision tree of prompts: each wave sends several\nquestions, and the answers to one wave select the next wave's questions."
        },
        {
          "modal": "Must",
          "text": "Every wave MUST report, as its reason, each of its questions with the\nquestion's id and text, Jev's choice and Jev's confidence."
        },
        {
          "modal": "Must",
          "text": "A wave the tree marks per statement MUST send one request for each MUST\nand MUST NOT statement of the record, carrying that statement with the\nwhole record as context."
        },
        {
          "modal": "Must",
          "text": "A per-statement wave's reason MUST list each statement's index and text\nwith each question's id, choice and confidence."
        },
        {
          "modal": "Must",
          "text": "A per-statement wave's verdict rule MUST be able to fire when any one\nstatement's answers match it."
        },
        {
          "modal": "Must",
          "text": "A per-statement wave's verdict MUST name the statements whose answers\nfired its rule."
        },
        {
          "modal": "Must",
          "text": "A per-statement request that gets no answer MUST fail with an error that\nnames its statement."
        },
        {
          "modal": "Must",
          "text": "A wave after the first MUST refine the answers of the wave before it."
        },
        {
          "modal": "Must",
          "text": "A wave after the first MUST be designed from the observed live results\nof the wave before it."
        },
        {
          "modal": "Must",
          "text": "A wave with no next wave MUST end the walk, with its verdict given by a\nrule in the tree file over that wave's answers."
        },
        {
          "modal": "Must",
          "text": "The tree MUST ship with the tool as a data file a consumer can override\n(REQ-JEV-0205); answers select the branch and the verdict, never a model\nchoosing the next questions."
        },
        {
          "modal": "Must",
          "text": "A failing answer MUST report the decision-tree path that failed."
        },
        {
          "modal": "Must",
          "text": "A live run MUST log every wave as output: its composed request, Jev's\nanswers, its reason and the branch or verdict they selected."
        },
        {
          "modal": "MustNot",
          "text": "The mode MUST NOT check structure that Raptor's import checks."
        }
      ]
    },
    "rationale": "Importing is part of complying with the schema: Raptor's import\ndiagnostics (REQ-RAP-0012) bring a document into the schema, then this\nmode judges whether each imported record is well written. Prose evaluation\nuses only records that pass both.\n\nRand (2026-11-08): \"for the 'is this adr/nfr/req high quality' mode, I\nwould expect this to be a decision tree of prompts. each layer of the tree\nwould output several questions which based on the wave-1 outputs would\nresult in a new set of questions to narrow in.\" On a dry run showing only\nwave 1: \"as long as real run logs all info as output (sc-agent will do logging and is\nalmost ready to start testing).\"\n\nRand (2026-11-08), after the first live run reported only fixed leaf text:\n\"every wave needs to provide some 'reason' the next wave is just to refine\nit. You can't design wave-2 until you see wave-1 results so you know what\nwave-2 should ask.\" The shipped trees therefore hold wave 1 only; a wave 2\nis designed from wave 1's observed answers.\n\nRand (2026-11-08) approved per-statement waves: one request per MUST / MUST\nNOT statement, with that statement plus the whole record as context, so a\nfailure names the statement to fix.",
    "success_criteria": {
      "text": "",
      "acceptance_criteria": [
        {
          "text": "Fixed answers to a wave select the same next wave every run.",
          "checked": false
        },
        {
          "text": "Every wave's result lists each of its questions with id, text,\nchoice and confidence.",
          "checked": false
        },
        {
          "text": "A live run's log holds each wave's request, answers, reason and\nselected branch or verdict.",
          "checked": false
        },
        {
          "text": "A REQ with two MUST statements and one SHOULD statement sends two\nwave-1 requests, one per MUST statement.",
          "checked": false
        },
        {
          "text": "A REQ whose third statement answers `check: none` reports `no`, names\nstatement 3, and lists every statement's answers in wave 1's reason.",
          "checked": false
        },
        {
          "text": "A dry run shows the composed wave-1 request for every statement.",
          "checked": false
        },
        {
          "text": "A record Raptor rejected is not sent to Jev.",
          "checked": false
        }
      ],
      "test_evidence": []
    },
    "dependencies": {
      "text": "",
      "requires": [],
      "related": []
    },
    "product_applicability": {
      "text": "",
      "applies_to": [],
      "does_not_apply_to": []
    },
    "implementation_notes": {
      "text": "Logging goes through the sc-observability logger (REQ-JEV-0008). A\nper-statement node sets `\"per\": \"statement\"`, `statements` (the record\nfield holding the statements) and `modals` (the modals judged); each\nstatement is one request whose item text is `{statement, record}`. The\nshipped REQ and NFR trees ask, per statement, `obligations`, `joined_by`,\n`term` and `check`; the ADR tree asks, per record, `context`,\n`alternatives` and `consequences`. Each option's criterion carries its fix\nhint, and the reason reports it.",
      "key_considerations": []
    },
    "test_strategy": {
      "text": "",
      "test_types": []
    },
    "related_documents": {
      "text": "",
      "requirements": [],
      "architecture_decisions": [],
      "design_documents": [],
      "work_items": [],
      "external_references": []
    }
  }
}

